WEBVTT

NOTE week03-clip3-vit-patches
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.675
A vision transformer, or V i T, reads
an image as a sequence of patches.

2
00:00:05.675 --> 00:00:12.417
Here is a pathology tile stained
with H&E, from a colorectal cancer.

3
00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:17.326
We cut the tile into a four by four
grid. That gives 16 square patches,

4
00:00:17.326 --> 00:00:20.950
and each patch is handled
separately from here on.

5
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:26.747
The 16 patches are lined up in reading
order, from top left to bottom right.

6
00:00:26.747 --> 00:00:30.417
Each patch now plays the
role of a word token.

7
00:00:30.617 --> 00:00:34.113
Each patch is flattened
into a list of pixel values,

8
00:00:34.113 --> 00:00:40.483
and a shared linear projection maps
it to a vector with d dimensions.

9
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:43.949
A position vector is added
to each patch vector.

10
00:00:43.949 --> 00:00:49.817
Without it, the model would not know
where in the image a patch came from.

11
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:54.709
A learnable CLS token is placed
at the front of the sequence.

12
00:00:54.709 --> 00:00:58.817
Its output will summarize the whole image.

13
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:03.386
These 17 tokens enter a
standard transformer encoder.

14
00:01:03.386 --> 00:01:06.507
Nothing in the encoder
is specific to images:

15
00:01:06.507 --> 00:01:10.150
it is the same architecture used for text.

16
00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:15.036
The output at the CLS position
goes to a small prediction head,

17
00:01:15.036 --> 00:01:19.350
which classifies the
tile as tumor or normal.

18
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:24.143
In practice, a 224 by 224 image

19
00:01:24.143 --> 00:01:26.780
with 16 by 16 pixel patches gives

20
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:32.313
a 14 by 14 grid: 196 tokens.
